<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Coverage settings. Pass this file to `dotnet test` with its settings switch, alongside the
  "XPlat Code Coverage" collector; `scripts/build.ps1` does.

  The exclusions are the point of the file. A number that counts generated resource accessors and
  the Electron shell measures the wrong thing twice: it cannot be improved by any test worth
  writing, and it hides movement in the code that can. What is left is the code ACT is expected to
  unit-test, which is the rules, the scheduler, the adapters, the stores and the services.

  What is excluded, and why each one is not a gap:

    * Act.TestSupport            : a fixture, not a subject. The mock adapter exists to drive other
                                   code; counting it flatters the total.
    * Act.App.Desktop.*          : the Electron window, tray and native dialogs. Deliberately not
                                   behind an interface (see repository-structure.md) because one
                                   quarantined class is cheaper than an abstraction whose only
                                   consumer would be a mock.
    * Act.App.Hooks.*,
      Act.App.Attachments.AttachmentEndpointExtensions
                                 : the ~100 lines that genuinely need ASP.NET, being an address, a
                                   middleware and three routes. Everything testable about the hook
                                   endpoint lives in Act.Infrastructure/Hooks and is counted; so is
                                   everything testable about the attachment route, which is
                                   TaskAttachment's image list and IAttachmentStore.ResolveInside.
                                   The extension method itself is the mapping and nothing else.
    * ServiceCollectionExtensions, Program
                                 : composition roots. A test asserting a container registration
                                   asserts the test's own copy of the wiring.
    * Act.Infrastructure.Power.* : SetThreadExecutionState and spawned helper processes.
    * *.g.cs, *.Designer.cs      : generated.

  Razor markup IS counted, as of 2026-08-05. It was excluded while `BuildRenderTree` was genuinely
  unreachable, on the note that covering it "needs a component host (bUnit) or a browser, which is a
  separate decision from this metric". That decision has since been taken — Act.App.UiTests hosts
  every component under bUnit — so the exclusion was hiding work that is really being done, and a
  `.razor` whose markup no test renders is now a gap the number reports like any other.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="XPlat code coverage">
        <Configuration>
          <Format>cobertura</Format>
          <Exclude>[Act.TestSupport]*,[Act.App]Act.App.Desktop.*,[Act.App]Act.App.Hooks.*,[Act.App]Act.App.Attachments.AttachmentEndpointExtensions,[Act.App]Act.App.ServiceCollectionExtensions,[Act.App]Program,[Act.Infrastructure]Act.Infrastructure.Power.*,[Act.Infrastructure]Act.Infrastructure.ServiceCollectionExtensions</Exclude>
          <ExcludeByFile>**/*.g.cs,**/*.Designer.cs</ExcludeByFile>
          <ExcludeByAttribute>Obsolete,GeneratedCodeAttribute,CompilerGeneratedAttribute,ExcludeFromCodeCoverageAttribute</ExcludeByAttribute>
          <SkipAutoProps>true</SkipAutoProps>
          <UseSourceLink>true</UseSourceLink>
        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>
</RunSettings>
